## 2. Two Layers of Traceability: Runtime Actions and Engineering Specifications

True institutional trust requires traceability at two levels: **every action the AI takes at runtime**, and **every architectural invariant the engineering team enforces at build time**.

{% cardgrid title="Dual-Layer Traceability Architecture" label="Traceability Layer" %}
- **1. Append-Only Database Enforcement.** Under strict least-privilege database role isolation, runtime service roles hold only `INSERT` and `SELECT` privileges on the audit ledger. `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, and `TRUNCATE` are revoked at the storage engine level.
- **2. Signed Object-Storage Anchors.** Completed audit partitions are periodically sealed with a detached `Ed25519` signature and anchored to WORM-compatible object storage, making even privileged database tampering detectable.
- **3. Canary Secret & PII Non-Disclosure Gates.** Automated evaluation suites inject synthetic canary secrets and regulated PII into mock tool outputs, verifying zero leaks into LLM prompts, logs, or unauthorized UI projections.
- **4. Specification-to-Test Traceability.** Normative architectural invariants across security, orchestration, and data contracts are mapped directly to automated verification suites and deployment qualification gates.
{% /cardgrid %}

---

## 3. Inspecting the Three-Tier Enterprise Qualification Framework

Explore how the reference architecture separates **deterministic core invariants** (verified automatically on every build) from **replay & fault-injection suites** and **target-environment attestation**:

{% blueprintledger /%}

{% boardcard title="Four auditability requirements before deploying enterprise AI" %}
1. **Correlate every hop from prompt to external receipt.** Require a single queryable chain linking `turnId`, `retrievalScopeId`, `proposalId`, `policyRevisionId`, `approvalId`, `grantId`, `operationId`, and `externalReceiptId`.
2. **Enforce append-only audit at the database role level.** Never give application service accounts `UPDATE` or `DELETE` privileges on the audit ledger, and hash-chain every row with `SHA-256`.
3. **Project tool outputs by audience (`resultPolicyHash`).** The LLM planner should only ever see the minimum redacted fields needed to compose the next step—never raw national IDs, IBANs, or salary bands.
4. **Track architectural compliance as executable code.** Decompose security and governance specifications into machine-verifiable invariants tested continuously in CI.
{% /boardcard %}
